":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nReproducible in 4.7.dev [220b0b2f7], 4.6.stable.\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.6.stable - Windows 11 (build 26100) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 Laptop - Intel i7-12700H (20 threads)\n\n### Issue description\n\nWhen `Use Snap` is disabled, holding `Ctrl` first and then `Left Click` to drag a 3D node will snap it to the grid. However, if `Ctrl` is then released (without releasing `Left Click`), the 3D node will still snap to the grid.\n\nThis issue does not happen when done in reverse:\n\nWhen `Use Snap` is disabled, holding `Left Click` first and then `Ctrl` to drag a 3D node will snap it to the grid. Then, if `Ctrl` is released (without releasing `Left Click`), the 3D node will stop snapping to the grid.\n\nThis may be related to https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/59687, since on rare occasions, after saving the scene, the node still snaps to the grid without holding down `Ctrl`. Pressing and releasing `Ctrl` fixes it.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create a scene and add a `Node3D`.\n2. Disable the `Use Snap` toggle in the toolbar.\n3. Hold down `Ctrl`.\n4. Hold `Left Click` to drag the Node3D. It should snap to the grid, as expected.\n5. Release `Ctrl`. It still snaps to the grid, which is not expected.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nN/A","closed_by":{"login":"akien-mga","id":4701338,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ3MDEzMzg=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4701338?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/akien-mga","html_url":"https://github.com/akien-mga","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/akien-mga/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/akien-mga/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://ap